%S A399901 1,2,3,1,4,1,2,2,3,3,1,4,4,2,1,5,2,3,4,3,4,1,5,5,2,6,3,1,2,4,3,5,4,5,
%T A399901 6,6,1,5,2,7,5,3,8,4,1,2,3,4,6,5,6,7,7,9,5,1,6,2,3,6,4,7,10,7,5,1,2,3,
%U A399901 4,6,5,7,11,6,7,8,8,12,1,13,2,6,3,4,5,7
%N A399901 Word representation of the precise grasshopper infinite barrycade (recursively defined family of permutations of the positive integers having pairwise disjoint sets of partial sums).
%C A399901 A barrycade is a collection of rows whose sets of partial sums are pairwise disjoint. In the infinite version considered here, each row is a permutation of the positive integers. Let B_phi = {phi_1, phi_2, ...} be the precise grasshopper infinite barrycade defined by the construction of A399900, with every earlier row regarded in its entirety as an infinite permutation. For n >= 1, a(n)=r if n occurs as a partial sum in row phi_r. The precise grasshopper construction is break-free, so every positive integer occurs as a partial sum in exactly one row. Hence a(n) is always a positive integer and records which row of the barrycade has a joint at position n.
%C A399901 The spelling "barrycade" is due to Richard Guy, who used it instead of "barricade" in honor of Barry Cipra, who prompted him to study the problem.
%H A399901 M. Dębski, J. Grytczuk, P. Naroski, B. Pawlik, J. Przybyło and M. Śleszyńska-Nowak, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18476">Finite and infinite barrycades</a>, arXiv:2609.18476 [math.CO], 2026.
%F A399901 a(n)=r if n belongs to S(phi_r), where S(phi_r) is the set of partial sums of row phi_r.
%F A399901 A399900(r) = min { n >= 1 : a(n)=r }.
%e A399901 The first four rows begin
%e A399901 phi_1 = (1,3,2,5,4,7,6,9,8,...),
%e A399901 phi_2 = (2,5,1,6,3,8,4,10,7,...),
%e A399901 phi_3 = (3,6,1,8,2,7,4,11,5,...),
%e A399901 phi_4 = (5,7,1,6,2,9,3,11,4,...).
%e A399901 Their initial partial sums show that positions 1,4,6,... belong to row 1, position 2 to row 2, position 3 to row 3, position 5 to row 4, etc. Hence a(1)..a(10) = 1,2,3,1,4,1,2,2,3,3.
%t A399901 A399901[n_] := Module[{u,s,p,init,step,extend,covered,r=1,a=ConstantArray[0,n]},covered[0,_]=False;covered[j_,x_]:=AnyTrue[Range[j],(init[#];extend[#,x];MemberQ[p[#],x])&];init[j_]:=If[!ValueQ[s[j]],Module[{m=1},While[covered[j-1,m],m++];u[j]={m};s[j]=m;p[j]={m}]];step[j_]:=Module[{k=1,q=1},While[MemberQ[u[j],k],k++];While[MemberQ[u[j],q]||q==k||covered[j-1,s[j]+q]||covered[j-1,s[j]+q+k],q++];Scan[(AppendTo[u[j],#];s[j]+=#;AppendTo[p[j],s[j]])&,{q,k}]];extend[j_,x_]:=(init[j];While[s[j]<x,step[j]]);While[MemberQ[a,0],init[r];extend[r,n];Scan[If[#<=n,a[[#]]=r]&,p[r]];r++];a]
%Y A399901 Cf. A399897-A399900, A399902-A399909 for other sequences arising from infinite barrycade constructions.
%K A399901 nonn,new
%O A399901 1,2
%A A399901 _Bartlomiej Pawlik_, Sep 23 2026
